# Ledgerline: repo-root ignores. Philosophy: commit small text/JSON run
# manifests + summaries; never commit per-run scratch (vegeta target fixtures,
# raw .bin attack logs, full DB dumps). What each rule keeps or drops is stated
# beside the rule.  The same set is what `CHECKSUMS.sha256` covers: its scope is
# `git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard` inside the run directory,
# so this file IS the publication scope rather than a description of it.

# --- results/ scratch ------------------------------------------------------
# Per-run IO scratch: vegeta targets + request bodies the runner stages before
# each attack. Big, regenerable, never a data source.
results/**/io/
# Capacity-gate request-body fixtures (staged outside io/, same scratch nature).
results/**/bodies/
# Raw attack logs / binary captures and DB dumps (regenerable, not published).
results/**/*.bin
results/**/*.dump
# Raw per-window traces: cgroup 50 ms cpu/mem samples + per-second p99 series.
# Tens of MB per run, regenerable-grade detail; the committed *.summary.json
# roll-ups carry the headline. Not published.
#
# THESE RULES ARE MACHINE-APPLIED. `bench/ledgerbench/checksums.py` takes the
# scope of `CHECKSUMS.sha256` straight from `git ls-files --cached --others
# --exclude-standard` in a run directory, so the checksum scope and the
# publication scope are one object rather than two lists that drift. It does not
# read this file, and does not subtract an exclusion set from a tree walk either
# (both were wrong, silently, in the same direction); git applies these rules,
# and whatever is in `.git/info/exclude` or a `core.excludesFile`, and its
# answer IS the map.
# Editing ANY rule here that a run directory can match changes what a published
# run verifies.
# The two run-id PREFIX rules at the end exclude a smoke/rehearsal run
# directory ENTIRELY, so git publishes nothing from those tiers and they carry
# no checksum map at all -- `verify` on one reports exactly that and exits 1,
# rather than exiting 0 having checked nothing.
# One rehearsal is tracked anyway, as the cross-tier replication check: its
# ledger, manifest and report were added by hand (see results/README.md), and
# it has no map either.
results/**/*.csv
# Same rule, same reason, for the /runtime-stats per-poll traces (W17): the
# `.runtime_stats.summary.json` roll-up beside each one carries both window
# edges and every delta, and IS committed.
results/**/*.jsonl
# Capacity-gate vegeta targets fixture (staged scratch, same nature as bodies/).
results/**/capacity-gate/targets.txt
# Keep: manifests, *.summary.json/*.warmup.json roll-ups, report/, ledger.json,
# verdict.json, doctor.json, CHECKSUMS: small text summaries committed.
# Rehearsal + smoke runs are never a data source: only full measured runs are
# committed (the hand-added rehearsal above aside). Both are matched by run-id
# PREFIX, which is why `_new_run_id` leads with the scale -- round 1 named runs
# timestamp-first, so this rule matched nothing and staged every rehearsal's
# artifacts for commit. Anything else under results/ is committed unless a rule
# above excludes it, so a future published run cannot be ignored by accident.
results/smoke-*/
results/rehearsal-*/

# --- atomic-write scratch (repo-wide, and in scope for CHECKSUMS.sha256) ----
# `util/paths.write_bytes_atomic` stages every artifact as `.<name>.<rand>.tmp`
# beside its target and renames. Its unlink-on-failure runs for Python
# exceptions only, not for the SIGKILL/power-cut class the resume protocol
# exists to survive, so a half-written one outlives a crash. A rule here keeps
# it out of the publication tree AND out of the checksum map in one move;
# excluding it in checksums.py alone would leave git committing a scratch file
# the map does not list, with `verify` green over it.
.*.tmp

# --- bench (Python runner) -------------------------------------------------
bench/.venv/
bench/**/__pycache__/
bench/.pytest_cache/
*.pyc

# --- services/dotnet build output ------------------------------------------
services/dotnet/bin/
services/dotnet/obj/
services/dotnet/Ledgerline.Tests/bin/
services/dotnet/Ledgerline.Tests/obj/

# --- blog working copy (local only) ----------------------------------------
# The narrative write-up is published separately on dev.to. Its
# Markdown source is a working copy, not a repo document, so it stays out of the
# tree; the figures it embeds are the committed docs/figures/*.png. Anchored at
# the repo root, so it cannot match inside a run directory.
docs/blog/

# --- editor / IDE ----------------------------------------------------------
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user

# --- OS noise --------------------------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
